---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/unemployment-labour-markets">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate lesson in Economics, and it builds on the subject's two
    foundational lessons: <em>Supply and Demand</em>, which introduced prices as the signals
    that clear a market, and <em>Money and Inflation</em>, which introduced aggregate demand
    and what a central bank does about it. Here the market being cleared is the labour
    market, wages are its price — and the puzzle is why this market so often fails to clear
    at all.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>compute an unemployment rate and explain what sits in its denominator — and why that makes the number gameable;</li>
    <li>distinguish frictional, structural and cyclical unemployment, and match each type to the policy family that addresses it;</li>
    <li>explain the natural rate of unemployment and why economists do not aim for zero;</li>
    <li>recount the evidence that wages are sticky, and why that turns a demand slump into layoffs;</li>
    <li>use Okun's rule of thumb to translate a jobs number into an output gap — and say where the rule breaks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Unemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labour force — where the labour force is those
    <strong>working or actively seeking work</strong> (the discouraged and retired don't count,
    which is why rates can fall when people give up). Economists separate three kinds:
    <strong>frictional</strong> (between jobs — healthy), <strong>structural</strong> (skills
    mismatched to available jobs — painful), and <strong>cyclical</strong> (recessions — the part
    policy targets). Behind the headline number sits a whole market: wages are its price,
    vacancies its demand side, participation its supply, and the flows between employment,
    unemployment and "outside" are what any single month's rate conceals. This page covers
    how the labour force is measured and gamed, the three types of unemployment and the
    natural rate, why wages do not simply fall to clear the market, what Okun's law links
    jobs to output, and the demand-side versus supply-side policy fork.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="fill the blanks — who counts as unemployed" widget="cloze" config={ueDefinitionCloze} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="the rate, its arithmetic, and Okun's rough law linking jobs to output" widget="sliderLab" config={unempCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> slide the unemployed count and watch the rate readout —
    then slide the labour force while holding unemployment fixed and watch the rate fall
    with nobody hired. That second move is the discouraged-worker effect the next section
    measures. Now watch the Okun estimate as the rate moves: each point above 4% swings the
    output gap by roughly two points — the exchange rate unpacked
    through this lesson.
  </p>

  <p>
    Okun's rule of thumb: each percentage point of unemployment above ~4% costs roughly 2% of GDP
    versus potential. The 4% figure is the "natural rate" — the frictional floor an economy
    never goes below without overheating.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the headline rate is one fraction with contested boundaries, and
    the natural-rate floor means even a healthy economy reports unemployment. Next: how
    statistical agencies draw those boundaries.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring: the labour force and its boundary games</h2>
  <p>
    The modern standard is the <strong>ILO definition</strong>, measured by survey (the UK's
    Labour Force Survey, the US Current Population Survey): <strong>unemployed</strong>
    means without work, <em>available</em> to start, and <em>actively seeking</em> in
    the past four weeks. The <strong>labour force</strong> is employed plus unemployed;
    the <strong>participation rate</strong> is the labour force as a share of the
    working-age population — the third number that keeps the other two honest.
    A rate can fall because people found jobs (good) or because they stopped
    looking (bad), and the two are distinguished only by reading participation
    alongside. The boundary choices create the measurement politics:
    <strong>discouraged workers</strong> (gave up searching — counted out),
    <strong>underemployed</strong> (part-time workers wanting full hours — counted
    as employed), <strong>involuntary part-timers and zero-hours workers</strong>
    (employed, in the statistic), and <strong>claimant counts</strong> (people on
    unemployment benefits — cheaper to collect monthly, but shaped by
    benefit rules, which is why most countries publish both survey and
    claimant measures). A serious reading of "the labour market" is a
    dashboard: unemployment rate, participation, hours, vacancies, and
    the <strong>Beveridge curve</strong> — the plot of vacancies against
    unemployment, whose shifts reveal how efficiently the market
    matches people to jobs (a Beveridge curve moving outward, as after
    the pandemic, means matching is worse at every level).
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="where does each person sit?" widget="sort-buckets" config={ueBoundarySort} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the unemployment rate counts everyone who does not have
  a job." It does not: the definition requires active job-seeking in the past four weeks, so
  retirees, students, carers and the discouraged all sit outside the statistic by design.
  That is why a serious reading pairs the rate with participation — the number that reveals
  whether the labour force itself is shrinking.</p>

  <h2>Three types of unemployment — and the natural rate</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Frictional unemployment</strong> is the between-jobs traffic: graduates
    searching, quitters relocating, matches taking weeks to form. It is
    not merely tolerable but functional — the search is how workers find
    better matches — and it sets a floor even in booming economies.
    <strong>Structural unemployment</strong> is the mismatch: skills, geography
    or regulation standing between workers and the jobs that now exist.
    Coal regions after pit closures; print journalists after the internet;
    regions whose industries moved away. Its signature is
    <em>duration</em> — long-term unemployment concentrated in places
    and trades — and its remedies are supply-side (retraining, relocation
    support, housing availability), not demand. <strong>Cyclical
    unemployment</strong> is the recession share: demand collapses economy-wide,
    and jobs go with it — the part that fiscal and monetary policy exist
    to fight, and the part Okun's law quantifies. The economy's floor is
    the <strong>natural rate</strong> (or <strong>NAIRU</strong> — the non-accelerating-
    inflation rate of unemployment): the level where the labour market is
    tight enough that wage inflation is stable, neither overheating nor
    depressed. It is estimated, not observed (central banks' estimates
    have drifted between roughly 4% and 6% in major economies), and
    treating a statistical estimate as a law has real costs — the
    2010s' "hysteresis" discovery showed that prolonged high unemployment
    itself <em>raises</em> the natural rate (skills atrophy, attachment
    breaks), which flips the old policy caution: idle workers are not
    just a symptom of weakness but a cause of future weakness.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each type with its example and remedy" widget="match" config={ueTypesMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one number, three different problems hiding under it — search,
    mismatch, and recession — each with its own duration and its own remedy. The next
    question is why the market does not simply price its way out of all three.
  </p>

  <h2>Why unemployment exists: sticky wages and matching frictions</h2>
  <p>
    In a frictionless textbook market, any temporary glut clears by price:
    wages fall until everyone who wants work finds it. Real labour markets
    adjust by <em>quantity</em> — jobs — rather than price, and explaining
    that is the field's central puzzle. <strong>Wage stickiness</strong> has
    accumulated foundations: explicit <strong>contracts</strong> (pay fixed for
    a year), <strong>morale and fairness norms</strong> (pay cuts sabotage
    effort and retention — the behavioural economics that earned Akerlof
    a Nobel), <strong>minimum wages and union agreements</strong> (legal floors),
    and <strong>insider-outsider dynamics</strong> (firms bargain with incumbent
    staff, not the unemployed). When wages cannot fall, a demand slump
    converts into layoffs instead of everyone earning slightly less —
    sticky prices, jobs as the shock absorber. The second pillar is
    <strong>search and matching</strong>: even in healthy times, workers and
    vacancies take time and cost to find each other (the
    Diamond–Mortensen–Pissarides framework, Nobel 2010). Search frictions
    explain why vacancies and unemployment coexist (the Beveridge curve),
    why <strong>unemployment insurance</strong> cuts both ways — longer,
    better-matched searches, but longer searches — and why policy
    arguments about benefits are really arguments about where on that
    trade-off a society chooses to sit.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="how a demand slump becomes layoffs" widget="order" config={ueStickyOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if wages simply fell far enough, unemployment would
  disappear — so the obstacle is greed or regulation alone." The sticky-wage evidence says
  the obstacle runs deeper: contracts, morale and fairness norms make pay cuts
  self-defeating for employers, so firms adjust by hiring fewer people instead. Wages do
  adjust — slowly — and the puzzle is precisely that they adjust far too slowly to stop
  jobs, not pay, from absorbing the shock.</p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the sticky-wage slip" widget="spot-error" config={ueStickySpot} />

  <h2>Okun's law: the jobs–output dial</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Okun's rule of thumb</strong> (Arthur Okun, 1962): each percentage
    point of unemployment above the natural rate corresponds to roughly a
    2% shortfall of real GDP from potential. The widget above computes it
    directly: at a 6% unemployment rate against a 4% natural rate, the
    estimated output gap is about 4% of GDP — hundreds of billions in a
    large economy. The law is a regularity, not a mechanism (the
    coefficient varies by country and cycle: 2 in the US, nearer 1–1.5
    elsewhere where hours adjust before headcount), and it works
    symmetrically in recoveries — rapid GDP growth first burns down
    unemployment, then raises output per worker. Its policy use is
    sizing: it is the arithmetic that lets a treasury price a recession
    ("this downturn will cost 4% of GDP — the stimulus is £X") and it
    explains why employment is always the last indicator to recover:
    firms exhaust hours, temps and attrition before hiring, and shed
    workers last.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the Okun output gap" widget="estimate" config={ueOkunEst} />

  <h2>Structural change hurts locally: the evidence</h2>
  <p>
    The aggregate rate is a national average over intensely local pain.
    The <strong>"China shock" research</strong> (Autor, Dorn and Hanson, AER
    2013) measured what import competition from China did to US
    manufacturing regions: affected commuting zones saw employment,
    wages and labour-force participation fall — and, crucially,
    <em>stay</em> depressed for a decade or more, with workers drifting
    to disability rolls rather than to growing sectors. Migration to
    opportunity proved far less fluid than the textbook assumed
    (housing costs, family, sunk local ties). The finding rewrote the
    trade-policy debate — aggregate gains from trade are real, and so
    is the decade-long concentrated cost — and it is the empirical
    template for automation debates: the question is never "jobs or
    no jobs" but which regions, which skills, and how long the
    transition takes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="diagnose a coal region — and pick the policy" widget="branch" config={ueChinaBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "low unemployment means the labour market
  is healthy — so the lowest possible rate is best." The dashboard says
  otherwise: below the natural rate, the economy overheats (wage-price
  spirals — the 1970s lesson), and a rate that falls only because
  participation collapsed is statistical recovery, not work. The healthy
  signature is a stable rate near its estimated floor <em>with</em> rising or
  steady participation, falling long-term unemployment, and a Beveridge
  curve that has not drifted outward.</p>

  <h2>Reading a labour market in four numbers, worked</h2>
  <p>
    One worked country-day shows the dashboard in motion. Population of
    working age: 52 million. Labour force: 34 million, of whom 31.3
    million are employed and 2.7 million unemployed. The <strong>rate</strong>:
    2.7 ÷ 34 = 7.9%. The <strong>participation rate</strong>: 34 ÷ 52 = 65% —
    the number that qualifies everything else (18 million outside the
    force: students, carers, retired, discouraged). Now the month moves:
    200,000 find jobs and 300,000 stop searching. Rate: 2.5 ÷ 33.7 =
    <strong>7.4%</strong> — down half a point. Participation: 33.7 ÷ 52 =
    <strong>64.8%</strong> — down. The honest read: the fall is real but
    flattered; a recovery strong enough to re-attract the discouraged
    would show the rate rising <em>again</em> as searchers return — the
    paradox that makes employment-to-population ratios (here 31.3 ÷ 52
    = 60.2%) the number economists trust most in prolonged
    recoveries, because it cannot be improved by people
    giving up.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="mark the unemployment rate" widget="place" config={ueRatePlace} />

  <h2>Seasonal and hidden: the categories the headline blurs</h2>
  <p>
    Two further categories complete the taxonomy. <strong>Seasonal
    unemployment</strong> follows the calendar — ski instructors in July,
    fruit pickers after the harvest, Christmas retail in January —
    and its policy treatment is smoothing: tourism economies build
    off-season trades, and statisticians publish seasonally
    adjusted series precisely so the news does not confuse
    December's hiring with a boom. <strong>Hidden (disguised)
    unemployment</strong> is the labour that is employed in name
    only: the underemployed part-timer wanting full hours,
    the over-qualified graduate in a stopgap job, the
    public sector carrying redundant staff to avoid the
    headline — in some economies, rural subsistence
    farming absorbs workers whose marginal product
    is near zero, which is why development economists
    discount official rates for poor countries. Both
    categories sharpen the measurement lesson: the
    unemployment rate is a narrow gate, and every
    serious analysis pairs it with the broader
    underutilisation measures — unemployed, plus
    underemployed, plus the marginally attached —
    that the statistical agencies now publish
    beside it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="two categories the headline blurs — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={ueHiddenCompare} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The unemployment rate falls from 7% to 5%, but mainly because hundreds of thousands of people stopped searching for work. Why is the fall less good than the headline suggests?</li>
    <li>A region built on coal mining stays jobless for years after the pits close, while new jobs in healthcare demand different skills in different places. Which type of unemployment is this, and which policy family addresses it?</li>
    <li>Use Okun's rule of thumb: with a natural rate of 4% and actual unemployment of 6%, what output gap does the rule estimate?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The denominator shrank — discouraged workers left the
    labour force entirely, so they count as neither employed nor unemployed; participation
    fell, and the recovery is weaker than the headline. (2) Structural unemployment — a
    skills-and-location mismatch whose signature is long duration; its remedies are
    supply-side (retraining, relocation support), not demand management. (3) Two points
    above the natural rate, times roughly 2, gives an estimated shortfall of about 4% of
    real GDP from potential — the sizing arithmetic a treasury uses.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Unemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labour force, and the labour force counts only those working or actively seeking — which makes the headline gameable.</li>
    <li>Read the rate with participation, hours and vacancies: a rate can fall because people gave up, not because they were hired.</li>
    <li>Frictional unemployment is healthy search; structural is a mismatch measured in years; cyclical is the recession share that demand policy targets.</li>
    <li>The natural rate (NAIRU) is the floor below which the economy overheats — and prolonged high unemployment can raise it, the hysteresis discovery.</li>
    <li>Sticky wages — contracts, morale, fairness norms, legal floors — make jobs, not pay, the shock absorber of a downturn.</li>
    <li>Okun's rule: each point of unemployment above the natural rate costs roughly 2% of GDP — a sizing rule, not a mechanism.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The output gap Okun's law estimates is the quantity <em>GDP and National Accounts</em>
    measures directly; the natural rate is the number <em>Monetary Policy and Central
    Banks</em> watch; and <em>Fiscal Policy and Public Debt</em> covers the spending side of
    fighting cyclical unemployment.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The Phillips connection.</strong> Unemployment and wage inflation
    trade off along the <strong>Phillips curve</strong> — the empirical relation
    that anchored macro policy until the 1970s stagflation (high
    unemployment <em>and</em> high inflation) broke its simple form. The
    modern synthesis: the trade-off exists short-run, shifts with
    expectations, and the NAIRU is its unemployment-side name. The
    2010s then embarrassed everyone again: unemployment fell far below
    estimated NAIRUs with little inflation, reopening the curve's
    steepness debate — the live question behind every central-bank
    rate decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Minimum wages: the empirical reset.</strong> The simple model
    (a floor above market wage creates unemployment) met the modern
    minimum-wage literature (Card and Krueger's New Jersey fast-food
    study and successors): moderate minimums in practice raised pay
    with small employment effects — monopsony power (employers
    setting wages below competitive levels) is one reason. The
    consensus is now dose-dependent: small rises, small costs;
    very large rises, untested. Card's shared 2021 Nobel marks the
    method's victory: natural experiments over blackboard models.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="true or false — the minimum-wage evidence" widget="judge" config={ueMinimumJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How will AI reshuffle the task composition
    of jobs — displacement at the speed of software, or the slow
    reallocation of every previous general-purpose technology? Do
    remote work and the gig economy make the labour force boundary
    unmeasurable (is a rideshare driver with three apps "employed"
    the way the statistic assumes)? And can active labour-market
    programmes (training, subsidised mobility) be made to work at
    scale — the evidence so far favours targeted, employer-linked
    programmes over classroom retraining, with effect sizes honest
    but modest. Behind all three runs the distributional question
    the aggregate rate was never designed to answer: which
    workers, in which places, carry the adjustment — and who,
    in a democracy, is accountable for them.
  </p>
